---
name: crew-recovery
description: The ladder for a Crew that stopped making progress - look once, one line, relaunch a dead pane, replace a confused Crew, report the failure - plus a Crew that never started and each refusal of `mate send`. Load it on a `blocked` item, when a Crew loops, stops responding, asks what its brief already answers, or shows a dialog or sign-in screen, and before you tell the captain a task failed. Covers Codex, Claude Code, pi and Grok.
---

# crew-recovery

`blocked` is the app speaking, not the Crew: the Crew can no longer report for itself.
You can do four things to a Crew: `peek` reads its pane, `send` types one line, `crew relaunch` restarts a dead one, `crew stop` ends it. There is no interrupt, escape, arrow or bare enter, and you never improvise a keystroke.
A Crew's harness is in `/usr/local/bin/mate crew list shop`; never assume it is yours.

## The ladder

Climb one rung at a time, and stop at the first that works.

1. **Look once.** `/usr/local/bin/mate state shop <id>`: `busy` means mid-turn, and a long quiet build is normal; leave it. Otherwise `/usr/local/bin/mate peek shop <id>` once. A dialog, sign-in, usage limit or model error is a failed start (below); the same action three times is a loop (rung 4); a question or a plan awaiting approval is rung 2; real progress is left alone.
2. **One line.** `/usr/local/bin/mate send shop <id> "<one line>"`. When the brief already answers it, point there: "The brief covers this: append the line at the end of the file, not the top." The captain's decisions are escalated as `wake` says, never guessed. A longer answer goes in a file in your directory, named by absolute path. If one specific line did not move it, a second will not.
3. **Relaunch a dead pane.** The agent is gone but the worktree, branch and status file are intact: `/usr/local/bin/mate crew relaunch shop <id> --note "<progress the dead pane held>"`. It starts the same profile in a fresh session that knows only the brief and status file. If it fails or the worktree is gone, rung 4.
4. **Replace a confused Crew.** `/usr/local/bin/mate diff shop <id> --stat` shows what it committed. Copy the `# Task` of `/ws/.mate/projects/shop/crews/<id>/brief.md` to `/ws/.mate/projects/shop/mate/briefs/<new-id>.md`, add `- Already tried (crew <id>): ...` lines above `Unknown:` (what it attempted, where it stuck, what not to repeat), and `brief check` it.
   `crew stop` the old one; if it refuses because work is unlanded, keep it and never reach for `--discard`.
   Spawn the replacement with the same `--repo` (and `--scout`). Its profile follows why the first failed: misjudged the work, one step heavier; its harness failed it while the work was on track, the same weight on another harness; unclear, the same profile. Move the old id out of the backlog with what happened and add the new one.
5. **Report the failure.** When the replacement fails the same way, the task has failed; a third Crew is not a plan. Record it with the evidence, tell the captain what was attempted, what happens and what would get further, and leave the branches unless they say to discard them.

A low context reading is not stuck; harnesses compact. Never leave a task silently stuck.

## A Crew that did not start

`crew spawn` answers the directory-trust dialog itself. A Crew still on a trust, sign-in, confirmation or usage-limit screen failed to start: never press keys at it.
Stop it (it committed nothing, so the stop is clean), spawn the same brief under a new id on the rule's other alternative with `--skip <harness>`, and record a perishable lesson so later spawns skip that harness until it works.
With no alternative left, try `default` on another harness once, then tell the captain which harness failed and what it showed, and keep the task queued.
`warning: <harness> quota is used up` from a spawn means the same, unless the captain chose that harness.

## When `mate send` refuses

Check a send's exit status before you record a line as sent.

- `is mid-turn`: leave it, or resend with `--queue` to wait behind the current turn.
- `has unsubmitted text in its composer`: the captain is probably typing there; leave it and say so.
- `showing a screen mate cannot name`, `a dialog is open`: peek, and start the ladder.
- `pane changed while it was being read`: send the same line again.
- `typed into ... but N enter(s) did not submit it`: retry the exact same command; the app checks its saved attempt against the draft and presses Enter without retyping. If it cannot verify the draft, peek and report; never clear or blindly resend it.

What follows is one part per harness, for telling what a pane shows; read only the Crew's.

## codex

Codex is the default Crew harness.

| Fact | Value |
| --- | --- |
| Busy signature | `esc to interrupt`, usually inside a `• Working (Xs • esc to interrupt)` footer |
| Idle composer | a bordered input box with the cursor in it |
| Directory trust | `Do you trust the contents of this directory?`, asked per absolute path |
| Instructions | the brief is delivered as a file in the worktree that Codex reads at startup |
| Model and effort | `--model` becomes `-m <model>`; `--effort` low to xhigh becomes `model_reasoning_effort`; `max` is recorded, not passed |

Codex confirms trust for each absolute path separately, and a Crew worktree is always a path it has never seen, so the dialog appears on essentially every spawn.
`mate crew spawn` answers it during launch and prints a `note: answered the codex directory-trust dialog for <worktree>` line on stderr when it did.
If you peek and the dialog is still on screen, the spawn did not complete: report that to the captain rather than sending a line at it, because a line typed into that dialog is not an answer to it.

A first prompt that Codex never picked up leaves the pane idle with the brief line visible in the composer.
That is a `pending` reading, and the fix is not to type: peek, then report it, because the Crew has not started and its worktree is empty.

## claude

Claude Code is what you run on, and what a Crew runs on when a spawn is given `--harness claude`.

| Fact | Value |
| --- | --- |
| Busy signature | `esc to interrupt` |
| Idle composer | a `>` prompt box |
| Directory trust | a trust confirmation whose default highlight is the refusing option |
| Instructions | the brief is delivered as a launch-time system prompt, so nothing lands in the worktree |
| Model and effort | `--model` takes an alias (`haiku`, `sonnet`, `opus`) or a full name; `--effort` takes low to max |
| Crew settings | a Crew loads the worktree's own `.claude/` settings and skills, never the captain's user-level skills, plugins or hooks; the captain's `apiKeyHelper` and `env` (from `~/.claude/settings.json` or `$CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR/settings.json`) are copied into the Crew's own `crews/<id>/settings.json`, so a Crew keeps the captain's auth path and proxy; it compacts at `crew.autocompact` of `.mate/workspace.yaml` (160000 tokens when unset, a negative number for never) |
| Mate settings | a Claude Mate also loads project settings only; each start sets the captain's `apiKeyHelper` and `env` in the Mate's own `.claude/settings.json`, keeping every other key there, so the Mate goes through the same proxy as its Crews |

The trust dialog's default highlight is the choice that exits the agent.
This is exactly why you have no key-sending command: a blind confirmation here kills the Crew rather than starting it.
Claude also shows a one-time bypass-permissions acceptance screen the first time a given configuration directory sees it; it looks like a dialog and reads as `unknown` the same way.

Claude renders a greyed-out suggestion inside an otherwise-empty composer after a turn ends.
It is not typed text and the classifier ignores it, but it is why a peek of an idle Claude pane can look like it has something in the box when `state` says the composer is empty; believe `state`.

## pi

A Crew runs on pi when a spawn is given `--harness pi`.

| Fact | Value |
| --- | --- |
| Busy signature | the line above the composer turns into `── ⠴ Working ───`, with a spinner cell that changes on every read |
| Idle composer | an empty row between two full-width rules, over two footer lines (the cwd, then usage and `(provider) model • thinking`) |
| Directory trust | `Trust project folder?`, only in a directory with its own `.pi/` resources; the launch passes `--no-approve`, so it should not appear |
| Instructions | the brief is appended to the system prompt by path at launch, so nothing lands in the worktree; the project's `AGENTS.md` still loads |
| Model and effort | `--model` takes `provider/id` (`deepseek/deepseek-flash`); `--effort` low to max becomes `--thinking`, which pi may clamp to what the model supports |

pi draws no prompt glyph: a draft starts at the left edge of the row under the first rule, and a turn in flight leaves that row empty under the spinner line, which is why `mate state` reads the spinner before the composer.
pi never asks permission before running a tool, so a pi Crew does not stop on an approval prompt the way it would on another harness.

pi lowers or raises the thinking level to what the model supports without saying so: a Crew asked for `--effort medium` on `deepseek/deepseek-flash` thinks at `high`.
The level it really used is the last word of the footer's second line, and the dashboard shows it for the Crew as its runtime effort once the first turn is recorded.

A Mate requires pi 1.0.4 or later. Mate explicitly loads its own extension with `-e` for prompt/answer receipts and recall, and its `.pi/skills` with `--skill`; automatic extension and skill discovery remains disabled.

A Mate initializes pi's native `createMcpExtension` with a workspace-only config loader and registers each plugin server with direct tool exposure. pi handles connections, OAuth (`/mcp`), tool results and shutdown. MCP server names replace `-` with `_` in tool names; global/project MCP files are ignored, so global servers cannot add tools or override workspace instances.

Plugin changes apply at the next start. Crews receive no workspace plugin servers or Mate hooks.

## grok

A Crew runs on Grok when a spawn is given `--harness grok`.

| Fact | Value |
| --- | --- |
| Busy signature | a braille spinner, a space and an ellipsis (`⠹ Waiting for response…`, `⠋ Thinking…`, `⠼ Responding…`), or the hint `Ctrl+c:cancel`, while the composer box stays empty |
| Idle composer | the bottom box, its first row `❯` and nothing else; the footer names the model and `always-approve` |
| Directory trust | the launch passes `--trust`, so a worktree is trusted without a folder dialog; the privacy banner above the box does not take keys |
| Instructions | the brief is inlined with `--append-system-prompt`; the project's `AGENTS.md` still loads, and so does the operator's home `Claude.md`, because the Crew uses the operator's grok home |
| Model and effort | `--model` when one is named; `--effort` low, medium, high and xhigh are passed, and max is left out |

Grok draws `❯` at the start of the composer box.
A turn in flight leaves that box empty, so `mate state` reads the spinner and `Ctrl+c:cancel` before the composer.
Lines in the scrollback also start with `❯`; only the box at the bottom is the composer.

Grok asks no permission before a tool when the launch passes `--always-approve`.
`--no-plan` keeps plan mode off, and `--fullscreen` pins the layout these screens were measured in.

A Crew inherits the operator's global hooks and the rules in the grok home, because authentication lives in that home and no flag was measured that turns them off.
A hook that fails prints a notice above the composer and does not take the composer.

A Mate cannot run on Grok yet.
Grok has hooks, but mate has not measured a hook whose stdin is what `mate hook` writes, and a Mate's memory and inbox rest on that.

These are the harnesses this binary was built with, each measured against the harness contract suite. Another harness is not registered, and a Crew on it could not be supervised.
